--- !u!114 &1205589924
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1205589920}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: af6c11f0d531f4249801ea5c2de41bcb, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ReadableTrigger
  interactionPrompt: Read
  backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5face2da520a6fa4fa8eff1c614aff70, type: 3}
  fontOverride: {fileID: 11400000, guid: e4cf2c21c93fda745b09b91b791a22c2, type: 2}
  overrideAlignment: 0
  textAlignment: 272
  overrideMargins: 0
  textMargins: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  overrideLineSpacing: 0
  lineSpacing: 0
  overrideFontSize: 0
  fontSize: 8
  pages:
  - "Excerpt from the Gravekeeper\u2019s Notes\nVanderbilt family\n\nThere is only
    one grave under the Vanderbilt name. The plot was selected by the deceased himself,
    James Vanderbilt. He arrived at the grounds in the late afternoon, alone. The
    paperwork was complete, neat, and signed without hesitation.\n\nJames Vanderbilt
    was an older man. Not much is recorded of him. His life was quiet, his presence
    unremarkable to most. He had a brother, though I have not seen him in years.
    The man is missing, and I am told little about where he has gone or why he has
    not returned.\n\nThose closest to James had grown distant in the final years.
    Whispers of resentment lingered around his household, small acts of cruelty or
    avoidance left unremarked. Whether these were deserved or not, they left him
    increasingly isolated, and the quiet around him grew heavier with each passing
    month."
  - 'The grave was tended without visitors. When asked if any other family might
    attend, no answer was given. There is no indication that James had any friends
    left who felt it necessary to be present.


    No additional details were requested.
    I was told that speculation was unnecessary.


    By the time illness struck,
    there was little to intervene. Letters, parcels, and small gestures of concern
    went unheeded or ignored. Whether from choice or neglect, the effect was the
    same.


    No objects have appeared at his grave, not even the small, deliberate
    tokens I have seen elsewhere. His stone bears only his name and dates, as though
    nothing else of him need be remembered.


    James Vanderbilt rests here alone.
    There is a space nearby for another, should circumstances change, though no instructions
    have been given.'
--- !u!64 &1205589925
