--- !u!114 &1241479726
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1241479722}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: af6c11f0d531f4249801ea5c2de41bcb, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ReadableTrigger
  interactionPrompt: Read
  backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5face2da520a6fa4fa8eff1c614aff70, type: 3}
  fontOverride: {fileID: 11400000, guid: e4cf2c21c93fda745b09b91b791a22c2, type: 2}
  overrideAlignment: 0
  textAlignment: 257
  overrideMargins: 0
  textMargins: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  overrideLineSpacing: 0
  lineSpacing: 0
  overrideFontSize: 0
  fontSize: 8
  pages:
  - "Excerpt from the Gravekeeper\u2019s Notes\nUnregistered visitors\n\nThere are
    two women who come to the gates when the light is low and the grounds are quiet
    enough to hear intent.\n\nThey never arrive together, though each insists the
    other has already been allowed through.\n\nBoth claim familiarity with the dead.
    Each knows names not written on stone. When asked how, one answers immediately
    and incorrectly; the other answers slowly and with details that do not improve
    clarity. I have learned that correcting either of them is a mistake.\n\nOne always
    asks about access. Locks, patrols, times when the soil is softest. She speaks
    of inheritance, of what is owed, of what should not be left unattended. When
    refused, she smiles as though refusal were expected. She returns anyway."
  - 'The other asks about boundaries. About what is held and what is trapped. She
    speaks of weight, pressure, the cost of keeping certain things in place. When
    refused, she looks surprised, then relieved, then disappointed. She does not
    argue. She simply adjusts her request the next time.


    I do not record which
    woman makes which request. It changes depending on who is listening.


    One
    of them is never permitted entry. This decision is simple and does not vary.
    She knows this and tests it regardless, offering reasons that contradict one
    another. Sometimes she claims permission already granted. Sometimes she claims
    necessity. Once, she claimed I had asked her to come.'
  - 'The other is allowed in under specific circumstances. Weather, time, and recent
    burials all factor into this. I do not explain my reasoning to her. When turned
    away, she accepts it as provisional. When admitted, she does not linger.


    Each
    accuses the other of dishonesty. Each provides evidence. Both sets are convincing.


    On
    one occasion, I asked what would happen if they succeeded. One said the grounds
    would be lighter. The other said they would be poorer. Neither elaborated.


    They
    continue to return. They never acknowledge one another directly. They never fail
    to mention the other by implication.


    I have learned that asking which one
    tells the truth produces only more visits.'
--- !u!64 &1241479727
